00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:14.250
 I'm here today with Jonah Raskin.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:19.250
 And Jonah is a writer, a poet, and a journalist with a love for food and the people who make it.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:33.250
 Jonah is the author of 14 books on subjects ranging from women in rock and roll, marijuana culture and politics, Jack London, Allen Ginsberg, and a personal exploration of food and farming culture of Sonoma County called Field Base.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:37.250
 One thread that connects these works is this loving and exuberant sense of place.

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:50.250
 His countless articles in the local press in Sonoma County, where we both live, very often profile eccentric characters, and quite often those eccentric characters are immersed in lifetime projects of drawing and making food.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.370
 Jonah was raised on Long Island.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:03:00.370
 He got a PhD in England, and he adopted Sonoma County in the late 1970s, where he taught literature and journalism at Sonoma State University.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.400
 I met Jonah at a community meeting on oysters in West Marin.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:11.400
 And some of my favorite writing of his explores the community's bitter controversy over the closing of the Drake's Bay Oyster Company.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.400
 He's currently working on a book about the drought in California.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.360
 So, Jonah, welcome to our podcast, The Delicious Revolution.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:22.030
 Great. Glad to be a part of it.

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.030
 Glad to be here and continue our conversation.

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:30.670
 So, I want to start talking about oysters, because that's how we met.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.930
 I'm an oyster lover.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:37.270
 I think you love oysters, too.

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:47.260
 And during that brief time that Kelsey and I got to live out in Inverness, she had a residency at the Lucid Art Foundation.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:56.640
 At that time, the community was totally embroiled in this controversy over the closing of the Drake's Bay Oyster Farm that had operated for more than 50 years.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.600
 And during that time, Point Reyes became a national park.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.600
 You did a lot of reporting on this.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:08.720
 What caught your interest about those oysters?

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:10.720
 That's a good question.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.720
 I'm trying to throw myself back to the beginning.

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:29.320
 I suppose the fact that there was this controversy about it and people were getting all heated up about it and it was in the courts and there were people on sides and people didn't want to talk to one another.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:43.020
 And I think that I was motivated to find out what was really going on there.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:49.020
 And by the time that I got interested in the oyster story, I had been writing about farming and about food.

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:59.020
 So it did seem like a natural extension of the kind of research and writing that I had been doing for years.

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:03.020
 I hadn't written about what I think they called mariculture.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:05.020
 I don't think I even knew the word mariculture.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:20.990
 And so it was a way to like to jump from farming on land to farming in waters and salty waters and to look at the oysters as a crop.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:30.540
 And so I don't really remember what I did first, though.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:47.510
 I do I do know that I spent a lot of time out in Drake's Estero where the Drake's Bay oyster farm was and talking to the people and and going out into the Estero with the oyster men,

00:05:47.510 --> 00:06:02.200
 including Jorge Mata, who is, I guess, the foreman and going out in different seasons and in winter and spring and summer and getting a feel for that place.

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:17.470
 And and learning, talking to them and hearing their stories and and I guess following the oysters from from there being harvested out in the waters

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:31.330
 and then brought in and then sorted and shucked and oysters put in jars and and then going to restaurants and talking to the chefs and people who are cooking with oysters,

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:48.880
 eating some oyster pizzas at the Osteria Stellina and going going to meetings and just talking to lots of people in the community about what was going on.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:57.230
 John, I do feel a sense of loss.

00:06:57.230 --> 00:07:00.250
 I'm sorry that the oyster farm is not there.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:11.340
 I wish that the federal government, the Department of the Interior could have figured out, well, how can we possibly have an oyster farm in a wilderness area?

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:22.200
 I don't think that they're innately, you know, not they can't cohabit or coexist.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:30.540
 There's there's there's there's no such thing as pure wilderness.

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.540
 There's no pristine wilderness.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:41.930
 Landscapes are socially constructed.

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:54.150
 I do know that there's people who like to like remove any trace of human habitation or where humans have worked or lived.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:59.490
 And but I guess that's not my perspective, not my point of view.

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:05.490
 I mean, people have been on the earth and have been in places that are called wilderness for thousands of years.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:07.490
 And we've we've altered the landscape.

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:18.970
 And there were Drake's Bay Oyster Farm was part of the culture and lifestyle for a lot of people who went out there.

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:22.490
 Yeah, I think we both easily came down on the same side of that.

00:08:22.490 --> 00:08:31.250
 But there's there was you talk to a lot of people who were who were adamantly against that letting that farm continue out there.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:42.230
 And it seemed to me and I think this is one of the things that you point out that they're like that specific controversy got right at the heart of what we mean when we talk about nature.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:46.350
 And especially out there in West Marin where there is there's a lot of nature.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:50.300
 Yes, there.

00:08:50.300 --> 00:08:51.300
 Yes, there is indeed.

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:57.300
 You do feel or I feel many people feel like you're their backs are to the ocean.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:00.300
 You can't go any further on land.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:08.460
 And I've heard people say who live there that it's the geography does like shape and influence a lot.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:14.840
 The way people look at the world and feel about the world is very rugged.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:18.840
 It can be it can be really windy and cold.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:26.840
 And if you go out to where the lighthouse is, you feel like you take a wrong step and you're in the ocean.

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.840
 The last time that I was out at Drake's Estero, it was a few months ago.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:34.840
 It was it was beautiful.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:35.840
 It was sunny.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:46.870
 There was no trace or almost no trace of of people who'd lived there and worked at Drake's Bay Oyster Farm that all the housing was gone.

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:50.870
 All the the the oyster shack was gone.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:53.870
 There was no boats.

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:55.890
 It was it was definitely beautiful.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:58.890
 And there was also there was nobody there.

00:09:58.890 --> 00:10:02.890
 No one at all there, which was also strange.

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:07.890
 I mean, to because every time I'd gone out there before there are people there who are working.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:24.380
 Yeah, you know, I'm kind of trained in ecology and it's it's been a shift in that field to think about nature as something as something that's that's real.

00:10:24.380 --> 00:10:36.230
 But it's not the same as something where we're not that we're living in an age that some some people are calling the Anthropocene where there's a where no part of the world is untouched.

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:40.290
 And we have to relearn what it means to do conservation in a world that's constantly changing.

00:10:40.290 --> 00:10:47.480
 And we have to relearn what it means to interact with nature when we can't just say nature is this thing that humans aren't.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:51.820
 Well, nature, yes, it's a big word.

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:57.820
 A lot of people capitalize capital N and in in nature.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:04.460
 I do remember being aware of the word and the concept from a very early age.

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:15.460
 I know that my father would always say and my father was took me and my brothers out into what we call the natural world of like hiking and exploring.

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:22.550
 And I think that's where a lot of my love of nature comes from.

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:28.510
 I can remember my father saying that human beings were a part of nature.

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:32.720
 And I know there are people who who are adamantly against that idea.

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:41.720
 Have you heard people like, you know, argue, argue and say, you know, we're we're outside nature where we're in antagonistic relationship to it.

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:46.200
 But we're also homo sapiens.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.200
 We're an animal species.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:12:01.380
 And we've we've we've we've fundamentally altered the the natural environment and in everything we've done from and from the beginning.

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:09.110
 In my own my most recent book is called a terrible beauty, the wilderness of American literature.

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:21.110
 And it's about the way that that Europeans and then U.S. writers, poets, novelists have described written about the stories they've told about the wilderness.

00:12:21.110 --> 00:12:40.580
 And I know that that that the word nature, which has been around for a long time, got a big boost around the time of the American Revolution, when people like Thomas Jefferson, the author of Declaration of Independence, start talking about natural rights.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:50.730
 Rights, rights, rights, rights that were inherent in being human, inalienable rights, like life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:13:09.250
 And I think that a lot what he and others meant was that these were their rights that didn't derive from society or from the class society of Europe and that were could be taken away by kings and aristocrats and royalty.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:20.150
 Now, Jefferson and others didn't they talked about natural rights, but they didn't talk also about the rights of nature, which people have.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:34.980
 I think that's a fairly new development that's come along that that's kind of related to ideas like thinking like a watershed and thinking like a mountain.

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:53.630
 And now with this growing awareness of climate change and I think people are increasingly aware of the necessity to to see the world from the perspective of nature itself.

00:13:53.630 --> 00:14:13.550
 Yeah, it's it's it seems like thinking about to me like thinking about food in in systemically the way that I think has been growing an area of thought that's been growing all over the place makes us rethink what we mean by nature.

00:14:13.550 --> 00:14:16.550
 It makes us kind of redefine that and yeah.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:25.280
 So but what did some of the people tell you who were who really wanted this oyster farm closed on the West Marine?

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:42.750
 I would say one of the strongest arguments for those who didn't like the oyster farm said, well, the Lonnie's Kevin and Nancy signed a contract and the cut and the lease was up and they knew this and it was time to go and they should have just gone.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:48.750
 And instead of trying to drag it out and extend it, I think for a lot of people just came down to the law.

00:14:48.750 --> 00:15:07.720
 And and and then also people complained that the oyster farm was polluting the waters and there was a lot of junk that was there and and that it was destroying the sort of destroying the natural beauty.

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:12.200
 I think those are like the two strongest arguments against it.

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:23.850
 I think I think in listening to a few people out when I was out there, there is this it was it was mostly an argument over like whether or not they should stay there.

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:33.200
 But there were these funny undercurrents or I heard some narratives that felt racist about so many Mexican workers being out there.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:44.950
 And there seemed like there was often an idea of the purity of that place that felt like it bordered on on something really ugly under the surface.

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:47.390
 That is possible.

00:15:47.390 --> 00:15:48.390
 That is possible.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:56.390
 I think, though, to a large extent, the the Mexicans who worked there were sort of invisible.

00:15:56.390 --> 00:16:10.610
 I mean, I know that I wrote about them and and I often would see pictures of the Mexican worker when there was a story like in the press Democrat to local newspaper around here.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:17.610
 There were pictures of them, but there there wasn't very much reporting about them themselves.

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:21.610
 I don't know that any any reporter actually went and talked to the Mexicans.

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:34.690
 So I think that there that they were they were at the heart of the of what was happening there because they were the ones who were doing the work.

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:43.720
 But in the stories that were written about the whole controversy, I think that they are for the most part ignored in and not included.

00:16:43.720 --> 00:16:44.640
 Yeah.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:46.170
 It is.

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:47.350
 It is.

00:16:47.350 --> 00:16:51.350
 It is a largely sort of white area, sort of West Marin.

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:52.350
 And there's a lot of ways.

00:16:52.350 --> 00:16:54.350
 It's like privileged.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:55.220
 Right.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:59.870
 And I have.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:13.870
 Yes, I've had heard some friends of mine say that the whole argument was like an argument between people who are largely well off and prosperous about what what they're going to what was going to happen in their backyard.

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:14.810
 Right.

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:16.000
 Right.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:16.970
 Yeah.

00:17:16.970 --> 00:17:20.090
 You've written a lot about people who think a lot about nature.

00:17:20.090 --> 00:17:22.090
 Jack London comes to mind.

00:17:22.090 --> 00:17:31.640
 So what was his contribution to this idea of nature and this idea of nature in the place that we live?

00:17:31.640 --> 00:17:40.610
 Well, Jack London's contribution, I would say, was a lot in his in the story of the novel.

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:45.610
 It's called The Call of the Wild, which was published in 1902.

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:57.580
 And I think that before then, to a very large extent, people thought when they thought about the wild in the wilderness, they thought about something that was external.

00:17:57.580 --> 00:17:58.580
 It was out there.

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:05.610
 And Jack London wrote the story in which the main character is a dog.

00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:07.610
 His name is Buck.

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:14.610
 And I think by writing about this dog, London was able to express a lot of his ideas about like human beings.

00:18:14.610 --> 00:18:18.610
 I mean, Buck is a very anthropomorphic dog.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:20.610
 He has all kinds of human feelings.

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:24.610
 And he's thinking, really a very thinking dog.

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:42.330
 So the wild is inside Buck and the wild that's inside him emerges when he's kidnapped from California and he's taken away to the Yukon.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:19:01.320
 So I think that London believed, and I think he also observed this, that there was what he might have called sort of reversions and people kind of animals and beasts sort of went back to their sort of original, whatever their original nature was.

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:08.320
 I believe that he could use the word atavism to describe it, to describe it.

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:10.990
 So there was that.

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:20.760
 The Call of the Wild has had a tremendous impact on people's thinking, I think, all around the world.

00:19:20.760 --> 00:19:29.760
 The best-selling book made into movies more than half a dozen times and published in more than a hundred different languages.

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:37.910
 So London also wrote about this part of the world, our part of the world.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:42.910
 One of his best novels is called The Valley of the Moon, which is another name for Sonoma Valley.

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:55.970
 And while the book is not mostly, it's not set in Valley of the Moon, it's the last section of the book takes place there.

00:19:55.970 --> 00:20:04.250
 And it's a kind of hymn to the sort of natural beauty of the Valley of the Moon.

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:14.500
 Jack London got there in 1905 and bought over 100 acres and he was part of what they called the Back to the Land movement.

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:22.500
 It had that name in the start of the 20th century and it's had a lot of different iterations in California history.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:30.500
 It does seem like people are always going back to the land in California and partially because there's land to go back to.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:32.500
 There's so much land here.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:42.720
 And it's a dream of a lot of people to go back to the land and what stands in the way now is the price of land.

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:50.710
 It's like prohibitive for so many people because the land is so valuable for grapes.

00:20:50.710 --> 00:20:58.540
 So that is, I think, one of the big challenges here.

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:08.600
 People in government, public life talk about the fact that farming is part of the identity of Sonoma County.

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:30.680
 So I think people that we need as a society here to not just give lip service to that idea but to figure out ways in order to provide farmland for people who want to farm and want to feed people.

00:21:30.680 --> 00:21:38.700
 Your book, Field Days, you gave me a copy when I met you and I read it the first couple weeks that I moved to Sonoma County.

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:39.700
 I loved it.

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:44.050
 It was like the perfect thing to fall in love with this place.

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:45.020
 Great.

00:21:45.020 --> 00:21:56.740
 It seems like that was your experiment in both like chronicling some people who are involved in a Back to the Land movement but also participating in it in some way.

00:21:56.740 --> 00:22:00.740
 So, yeah, can you talk a little bit about that book and that experience?

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:19.200
 Well, as a reporter journalist, I've never accepted the dichotomy between the observer and the participant and I've like participated in a lot of stories that I've written and I think it's, you know, added to the stories that I've written.

00:22:22.480 --> 00:22:30.480
 My first idea was to do a book of photographs and to do a book of photographs about a farm which is down the road for me.

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:32.470
 It's called Valley End.

00:22:32.470 --> 00:22:44.840
 I would drive past it a couple times a day and I would just look at it, the fields and the landscape and it's literally at the end of the valley.

00:22:44.840 --> 00:22:51.730
 And there was a, they had a farm stand there and I used to stop and talk to the guy who was selling.

00:22:51.730 --> 00:23:00.800
 So, I talked to my friend, Paige Green, who's a photographer and about taking pictures and she said, "Yes, definitely."

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:07.830
 So, we met on Petaluma Hill Road, right on the shoulder of the road and I asked her to start taking photographs.

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:09.830
 And she said, "Well, I can't do that.

00:23:09.830 --> 00:23:13.830
 I mean, we have to go and get permission of the farmer before that."

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:16.790
 So, I didn't really like that idea.

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:18.790
 I just wanted to take the pictures.

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:23.110
 But I could see her point of view.

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:30.230
 So, we left our cars there and we walked down this sort of long driveway to the farmhouse in the distance.

00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:36.230
 And right then and there, I felt myself like I was no longer outside the landscape that I've been looking at.

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:41.320
 But now I was inside the landscape and I was a part of it.

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:59.180
 And then when we were let into the house and met Sharon Grassi, who was a farmer and heard her stories, I realized that the book had to be more than just photographs.

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:03.330
 It also had to be the stories of the farmers themselves.

00:24:03.330 --> 00:24:15.290
 And I think it was probably significant that at the very beginning, the start of this odyssey across this landscape, that I did talk to a woman farmer.

00:24:15.290 --> 00:24:20.290
 And she had the help of her son, Clint, but she didn't have a husband.

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:29.400
 And then I also met the Mexican crew, I don't know, the first week that I was there and talked to them.

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:39.430
 And if I had stereotypes of Mexican field workers, I was soon, I soon lost them.

00:24:39.430 --> 00:24:48.430
 I mean, I remember that they were concerned about food and health and they didn't want to work in places where there are chemical sprays.

00:24:48.430 --> 00:24:56.710
 And they would talk about their own food issues and diabetes and having to eat right.

00:24:56.710 --> 00:25:08.190
 And so they were savvy and they were very eloquent in the things that they talked about.

00:25:08.190 --> 00:25:09.250
 Yeah.

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:14.900
 And you spent a year going there, is that right, participating in that farm?

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:20.410
 I was there and then I was also, I spent a lot of time at Oak Hill Farm.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:21.410
 Right.

00:25:21.410 --> 00:25:24.410
 I mean, Oak Hill was the main farm where I spent a lot of time.

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:31.470
 I had been to a number of farms and then a friend of mine said, well, if you're writing about farms, you have to go to Oak Hill.

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:35.470
 And I had never heard of Oak Hill, which is in Glen Ellyn.

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:40.950
 So I went there, I met the woman who owns the farm, Ann Teller.

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:58.050
 She drove me around in her pickup truck and I saw the farm, all the different things that are going on there, the growing flowers, berries, fruit trees, row crops.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:07.850
 It had the Red Barn store where they sold produce and there was like over 20 people who are living in different houses there.

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:10.290
 She was living there.

00:26:10.290 --> 00:26:17.290
 And I remember saying to her, well, if I'm going to write about Oak Hill, I'm going to need to actually work here.

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:21.290
 And it's the only way I can see myself telling the story.

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:26.290
 And she said, well, OK, but talk to the farmer, talk to Paul Wertz and see what you can arrange.

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:30.450
 So I found Paul and I said, I want to work here.

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:33.450
 And he looked at me very dubiously.

00:26:33.450 --> 00:26:35.450
 Why does this guy want to work?

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:36.450
 I said, it's hard work.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:38.340
 Why does he want to work on a farm for?

00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:43.740
 So he said, OK, yeah, if you want to if you want to work, show up tomorrow morning at 630.

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:48.960
 So I didn't even have time really to like go back and forth in my head.

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:53.960
 It was like it was right there upon me.

00:26:53.960 --> 00:26:56.960
 So I was living here in this old farmhouse.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:57.960
 I set my alarm for 530.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:12.710
 I got dressed and drove over Grange Road to Crane Canyon to Bennett Valley and and then over to 12.

00:27:12.710 --> 00:27:19.080
 And I worked that very first day with the crew of all of all Mexicans.

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:26.080
 And then I sort of continued to do that all through the different seasons of that year.

00:27:26.080 --> 00:27:30.250
 How did that first day feel?

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:32.250
 Well, it was really exhausting.

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:37.220
 By the end of the day, I could barely like stand up straight.

00:27:37.220 --> 00:27:43.380
 I mean, I was a lot of bending down and getting close to the ground and lifting things.

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:48.340
 It was it was it was on a very steep learning curve.

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:51.340
 It was kind of looking in hindsight.

00:27:51.340 --> 00:28:04.410
 It feels kind of funny because Paul words told me just to meet the crew in front of the barn and then to go with them.

00:28:04.410 --> 00:28:09.630
 And at that time, my Spanish was rudimentary.

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:10.630
 It did get better.

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:13.630
 And their English was they did.

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:15.630
 It was rudimentary.

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:19.630
 And at one point, they just got in the pickup truck and started to drive off.

00:28:19.630 --> 00:28:22.630
 And nobody had said, we'll get in the truck or we're going now.

00:28:22.630 --> 00:28:29.630
 And so I kind of ran uphill and jumped in the back of the truck and then and then went up there with them.

00:28:29.630 --> 00:28:36.660
 And then they got out of the truck and they started harvesting squash and zucchini.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:39.630
 And and nobody again, nobody said anything to me.

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:44.630
 And then I said, Joquero and, you know, a knife.

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:49.170
 They had these large knives that they were using.

00:28:49.170 --> 00:28:54.230
 So I started harvesting and then everybody kind of got into the act.

00:28:54.230 --> 00:29:00.230
 And if I would harvest something that was too small, I would say, no, no, no, no.

00:29:00.230 --> 00:29:04.230
 Or sometimes sometimes I would be too large, you know.

00:29:05.230 --> 00:29:09.230
 So, yes, everybody was like telling me how to do things.

00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:23.350
 And I imagine that they are dubious of me like at the beginning, why I was there and why did I want to do this.

00:29:23.350 --> 00:29:30.350
 But they got to accept me and invite me into the tribe and to be part of their world.

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:39.310
 And they would their wives or girlfriends would bring food with them to eat like at a break at nine o'clock or so.

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:47.420
 And they would share with share the food and I would hear their stories.

00:29:47.420 --> 00:29:58.640
 And I mean, there was clowning around and a lot of playfulness thing.

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:00.640
 There were things that surprised me.

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:10.640
 I was on the went with the flower crew different than the vegetable crew and harvesting all kinds of vegetables, including the sunflowers that were like eight or 10 feet tall.

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:13.640
 It was like being this forest of sunflowers.

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:24.850
 And I was also like kind of, I would say, nervous about how to like harvest the flowers the right way.

00:30:24.850 --> 00:30:32.850
 So I was asking Jesus, you know, well, do I do I count the number of flowers or is it a matter of hand eye coordination?

00:30:32.850 --> 00:30:40.850
 Or he said, well, it's just the feel you get to know the feel of you don't count them and what they feel like in the palm of your hand.

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:45.010
 And like and he said, well, he said, I do it this way.

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:48.010
 But if you want to do it differently, you can do it differently.

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:50.980
 And then he gave me a quotation in Spanish.

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:55.980
 It was cada maestro tiene su librito.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:57.980
 Every master has his own book.

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:01.980
 So if you have if you have your way, go ahead and do it your way.

00:31:01.980 --> 00:31:02.980
 Yeah.

00:31:02.980 --> 00:31:10.730
 So that that was that was eye opening.

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:21.590
 One day where we'd harvested the the we harvested the the flowers and they were in the back of this flatbed truck.

00:31:21.590 --> 00:31:29.590
 And a couple of the guys started decorating this truck with flowers all around the cabin or just around the whole thing.

00:31:29.590 --> 00:31:42.140
 And that really surprised me are these sort of Mexican workers who obviously have an aesthetic sense or or care about beauty.

00:31:42.140 --> 00:31:44.230
 And their flower.

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:45.230
 Yeah.

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:48.230
 The way they around the range of flowers are beautiful.

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:49.730
 Yeah.

00:31:49.730 --> 00:32:03.180
 I think a lot of what comes out in writing in that book and in a lot of short pieces about people around Sonoma County is this strong sense of these people is like an exploration of individual people,

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:04.180
 not just what's happening.

00:32:04.180 --> 00:32:11.500
 And I guess I'm just thinking about looking around your living room where we are and we're surrounded by the the beats.

00:32:11.500 --> 00:32:14.500
 There's Ginsburg and Kerouac and Burroughs.

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:15.500
 And so.

00:32:15.500 --> 00:32:19.780
 So that sensibility seems.

00:32:19.780 --> 00:32:30.730
 And, you know, it seems like you have this sensibility of diving in of exploring these characters as part of as part of the joy of that reporting.

00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:33.070
 Right.

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:34.070
 Well, yes.

00:32:34.070 --> 00:32:50.550
 I mean, the beat writers, especially Ginsburg and Kerouac did sort of value individual experience and and including themselves in in their work, whether it was on the road or how.

00:32:50.550 --> 00:33:03.550
 And they're using the first person pronoun I and like Ginsburg and how I saw the best minds of my generation destroyed by madness, starving, hysterical, naked.

00:33:03.550 --> 00:33:10.510
 And so I think they helped to give me permission to include my own experience and tell it from my point of view.

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:27.220
 And so I started to read the beats when I was a teenager and I've been reading and read rereading them and writing about them and teaching them.

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:35.330
 So I think it probably like sinks in and has influenced me even in ways I'm not aware of.

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:37.350
 Yeah.

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:54.160
 So that that realization that there's an aesthetic sense even on a production in the midst of a production farm by people who are seen as doing the productive work that feels like an important insight into how things work and food and how things work here in Sonoma County.

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:55.160
 Yes.

00:33:55.160 --> 00:33:56.150
 Yeah.

00:33:56.150 --> 00:33:57.150
 Yeah.

00:33:57.150 --> 00:33:58.150
 Yeah.

00:33:58.150 --> 00:34:01.150
 They definitely cared at Oak Hill about the appearance of the food.

00:34:01.150 --> 00:34:05.150
 I mean, one of the things that happened was like we would eat food right in the in the field.

00:34:05.150 --> 00:34:12.150
 I mean, we would like if somebody we were harvesting carrots and somebody was hungry or someone just take a carrot and just wipe some of the dirt off.

00:34:12.150 --> 00:34:17.150
 I mean, there was still dirt on it and just eat eat a carrot right there in the field.

00:34:17.150 --> 00:34:24.500
 And I don't think I would have done that before this experience because I thought, well, it's dirt.

00:34:24.500 --> 00:34:25.500
 I don't know.

00:34:25.500 --> 00:34:28.500
 You have to wash it and peel it.

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:31.500
 So nothing bad ever happened.

00:34:31.500 --> 00:34:50.490
 You know, there's nothing cleaner than this organic dirt there at Oak Hill or times in the summer when Miguel would get out his knife and he would get a ripe melon and open the melon up and just have this burst of aroma and eat a melon standing there in the field.

00:34:50.490 --> 00:35:05.710
 So I did wonder a lot of times about the the the carrots because the carrots had to look really beautiful in order to get taken out of the field and put into boxes.

00:35:05.710 --> 00:35:08.710
 If they if they looked weird, they stayed in in the field.

00:35:08.710 --> 00:35:14.860
 I mean, there's a lot of sometimes I thought, you know, wasted carrots there.

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:17.860
 I mean, they got turned over and they went back into the earth.

00:35:17.860 --> 00:35:27.620
 I mean, there are there's other farms where people are not so it doesn't have to look like absolutely beautiful.

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:29.000
 Yeah.

00:35:29.000 --> 00:35:32.320
 How long ago was your was your year at that farm?

00:35:32.320 --> 00:35:35.720
 Oh, this is like in 2007 2008.

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:40.710
 I believe the book came out in 2009.

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:53.400
 And it did seem like the back to the land, the farming movement had by the time the book came out, reached a kind of plateau.

00:35:53.400 --> 00:36:03.930
 So the farming world seemed like it was it was kind of fixed in place and it wasn't clear like what was going to happen or whether it would change.

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:09.520
 And it was there was just a kind of static time.

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:19.520
 And then soon after that, it started to be in flux again because there were there were young farmers who were coming from other parts of the country.

00:36:19.520 --> 00:36:24.520
 And some of them with it with rich background and experience who wanted to farm here.

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:36.860
 And there was some the kind of the legend of Sonoma County of partially because of Bob Kennard, who's at Green String and and also Paul Wertz.

00:36:36.860 --> 00:36:43.610
 And so there were other farms that started popping up.

00:36:43.610 --> 00:36:51.570
 And when I was exploring and writing, most of the of the farmers were growing vegetables and some and fruits.

00:36:51.570 --> 00:37:05.570
 And a lot of the new people who came in at the start of the second decade of the 21st century were raising chickens and goats and pigs and cows and making cheese.

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:10.850
 And you could eggs and bacon.

00:37:10.850 --> 00:37:14.810
 And and so it turned that way.

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:24.830
 And I would think sort of more biodynamic farming and and then the creation of organizations.

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:34.830
 Well, like the Farmers Guild and then reinventing the the Grange, which has been around for a long time, but been a kind of moribund.

00:37:34.830 --> 00:37:39.860
 A kind of moribund institution and reinvigorated and like in Sonoma.

00:37:39.860 --> 00:37:44.860
 The Grange has been renovated and new members and younger people.

00:37:44.860 --> 00:37:52.460
 And so it's the farming the farming scene right now.

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:53.460
 It's kind of very lively.

00:37:53.460 --> 00:37:56.460
 This is just there's a lot that's going on.

00:37:56.460 --> 00:38:03.970
 I live right next door to a place that's been a farm.

00:38:03.970 --> 00:38:11.940
 Well, farm working horse organic agriculture where they've grown vegetables and they're going to be that that is closing down.

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:20.900
 But there are there's some people who raise sheep and make cheese who are going to be next door.

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:26.380
 And they just put in a huge fence off a huge pasture for the sheep.

00:38:26.380 --> 00:38:29.380
 And they're just putting up this a barn for the sheep.

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:37.310
 So I'm really excited to see this sort of next iteration of farming right next door to me.

00:38:37.310 --> 00:38:47.700
 I think there's this story that sometimes told about going back to the land like you managed like you mentioned in Jack London's time and then in the 70s.

00:38:47.700 --> 00:38:57.710
 And then and then now as I think sometimes the story goes that there's these waves of a of a of it being in style and then it goes out of style.

00:38:57.710 --> 00:39:00.830
 And sometimes I worry that that's what's happening.

00:39:00.830 --> 00:39:05.890
 But also I feel these these strings of connectivity between those those times.

00:39:10.730 --> 00:39:18.100
 Well, yes, there's a certain amount of ebb and flow.

00:39:18.100 --> 00:39:29.410
 And my parents got to Sonoma County in the 70s and they were farming organically and mostly they didn't have cash crops, though they did.

00:39:29.410 --> 00:39:39.630
 They had berries like boysenberries and raspberries that my father would sell at the they would call them, you know, natural food stores in those days.

00:39:39.630 --> 00:39:42.850
 And there was a couple around.

00:39:42.850 --> 00:39:47.110
 There was one in in Monte Rio and there was one in Cotati.

00:39:47.110 --> 00:39:55.490
 And and there were other there were other farms around and a lot of them didn't make it.

00:39:55.490 --> 00:40:06.240
 I mean, I think you have to have in order to succeed organically farming, you have to have farmers markets.

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:19.540
 And it helps to have like restaurants and chefs who want to have like buy organic food and like even say on the menu that the produce comes from green string or from Paul's produce.

00:40:19.540 --> 00:40:20.540
 And you have to have markets.

00:40:20.540 --> 00:40:26.950
 And so it took a while to for the markets to develop.

00:40:26.950 --> 00:40:33.880
 So I think it's still it's still really hard.

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:34.880
 It's still really challenging.

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:39.880
 People people, people labor long hours.

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:41.880
 And I think it's a lot.

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:44.840
 It's it's really satisfying for a lot of people who do it.

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:52.840
 A lot of people tell me or Paul told me Paul was once he told me that there was something magical about farming.

00:40:52.840 --> 00:41:04.840
 I mean, there's a field and you plow it and it's been fertilized and you go through and you plant seeds and you don't know for sure if what's going to happen.

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:10.830
 All of a sudden, the seeds come up and then you've got you've got tomato plants.

00:41:10.830 --> 00:41:21.850
 And a few months later, you've got thousands of tomatoes and is this incredible productivity and fecundity of this earth.

00:41:21.850 --> 00:41:25.850
 And so it does it does seem like magic.

00:41:25.850 --> 00:41:27.850
 And I've told that story to other people.

00:41:27.850 --> 00:41:30.850
 And they've said, yeah, the same thing.

00:41:30.850 --> 00:41:37.660
 You know, you've been you told me you've been working on a book about the drought here in California.

00:41:37.660 --> 00:41:38.660
 Yeah.

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:39.660
 Yeah.

00:41:39.660 --> 00:41:42.620
 And so I imagine you've been talking to a lot more farmers about that.

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:45.550
 Yes.

00:41:45.550 --> 00:41:46.550
 Yes.

00:41:46.550 --> 00:41:53.830
 I've been talking to to all kinds of farmers and ranchers and grape growers and winemakers.

00:41:53.830 --> 00:42:10.380
 And most people seem to feel like, yes, the water and drought is it affects everybody in the world of agriculture or any in all walks of life in Sonoma County.

00:42:10.380 --> 00:42:13.890
 So it isn't it's an ongoing story.

00:42:13.890 --> 00:42:16.890
 We've been in this is the fifth year of a drought.

00:42:16.890 --> 00:42:20.890
 I think people are aware that even though it's rained, we're still in the drought.

00:42:20.890 --> 00:42:32.000
 And we don't know when we're going to get out of the drought because we've had lowering of water tables because because we've been pumping water out of the ground.

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:35.000
 We don't have a lot of the streams.

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:40.000
 There's no water in the streams or there's less in stream flow.

00:42:40.000 --> 00:42:45.980
 We've had loss of water in reservoirs.

00:42:45.980 --> 00:42:57.980
 And we've all become aware of the need to conserve water and the sort of growing sort of water literacy, this awareness that water is a finite resource.

00:42:57.980 --> 00:43:02.170
 It's there's not unlimited supplies of it.

00:43:02.170 --> 00:43:14.430
 So I in some ways, I think the book about the drought is like a continuation of field days.

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:17.430
 It probably has me in it again.

00:43:17.430 --> 00:43:27.980
 It has journeys around the landscape of Sonoma County in different places.

00:43:27.980 --> 00:43:39.420
 And it also has institutions that are part of the water, the hydrological picture, like the Sonoma County Water Agency and different water groups.

00:43:39.420 --> 00:43:46.390
 The Sonoma County Water Coalition and the Water Institute out in Occidental.

00:43:46.390 --> 00:43:51.380
 And there's a lot of people who have been thinking and writing about water and about drought.

00:43:51.380 --> 00:44:03.630
 And so it's also it's putting together all these different voices that people have to say about water and drought here.

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:13.240
 I don't want to say that Sonoma is a microcosm of California.

00:44:13.240 --> 00:44:14.240
 I don't know whether it is.

00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:27.420
 California is a big place is there's a lot of different regions and landscapes and probably there's so many different stories depending on where you are.

00:44:27.420 --> 00:44:34.420
 If you're near the Mexican border or up near Oregon or on the coast or east of the Sierras.

00:44:34.420 --> 00:44:43.420
 So I know there's a there's an inclination on the part of authors and on their editors to say, well, why are you writing?

00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:48.420
 You know, how is the story of this place like emblematic of some kind of larger story?

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:57.420
 And there there are links between or similarities between the drought here and drought other places.

00:44:57.420 --> 00:44:59.420
 But it's also really unique.

00:44:59.420 --> 00:45:07.420
 And I want to tell the sort of just the unique quality of the drought here as in the human experience of the drought.

00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:08.420
 What it means.

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:11.350
 What does it actually mean to live through a drought?

00:45:11.350 --> 00:45:14.420
 There are not as far as I can see a whole lot of books about drought.

00:45:14.420 --> 00:45:21.890
 There's a lot of books about water and and about rain drought.

00:45:21.890 --> 00:45:34.680
 In some ways, drought, it's is harder to write about or more challenging to write about because, I mean, you can see it when it rains and when it floods.

00:45:34.680 --> 00:45:42.020
 So sometimes looking for drought is like more subtle.

00:45:42.020 --> 00:45:47.880
 There's a place really close to me where I was exploring and the ground is all cracked.

00:45:47.880 --> 00:45:56.350
 You know, the way you're used to seeing in places where India, for example, where there's severe drought.

00:45:56.350 --> 00:46:04.410
 And I went out with a photographer, a friend of mine, and we took some pictures of me and this cracked earth.

00:46:04.410 --> 00:46:12.500
 Yeah, it seems to me that there's a couple of things that stick in my mind when I think about drought in California.

00:46:12.500 --> 00:46:20.760
 And one is that, you know, there's where risk of much greater drought in the future.

00:46:20.760 --> 00:46:22.790
 And we just had this big climate deal in Paris.

00:46:22.790 --> 00:46:30.290
 And there's been a lot of thought put into what to do about it, about how to adapt and what to do about climate change itself.

00:46:30.290 --> 00:46:36.470
 But there's also, I think, a lot about John Steinbeck in East of Eden.

00:46:36.470 --> 00:46:44.820
 In that beginning, he's talking about the Salinas Valley, but he says the dry years last usually just long enough for you to forget about the west years.

00:46:44.820 --> 00:46:49.880
 And then it starts to rain and then the wet years last long enough for you to forget about the dry years.

00:46:49.880 --> 00:46:56.940
 And it's just that indication of our long history of a drought and the weird ways that we forget about it.

00:46:56.940 --> 00:46:58.730
 Yes, right.

00:46:58.730 --> 00:46:59.730
 Yeah, yeah, it does.

00:46:59.730 --> 00:47:00.730
 That is curious.

00:47:00.730 --> 00:47:10.720
 The way I guess people don't want to remember droughts because why don't they want to remember droughts?

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:14.720
 I don't know, because there's sort of hardship and deprivation of people.

00:47:14.720 --> 00:47:17.720
 And they're just, it's just sort of worrisome, you know.

00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:21.720
 And there's good reason why people are worrying.

00:47:21.720 --> 00:47:23.720
 There are places where wells have run dry.

00:47:23.720 --> 00:47:32.720
 I mean, for individuals and also for towns like East Porterville or people having to cut back really severely.

00:47:32.720 --> 00:47:41.720
 Or, I mean, right here, just a 10-minute drive away from me, Green String Farm ran out of water in August.

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:52.140
 So it's, and I spent a day with Bob Kennard in August.

00:47:52.140 --> 00:47:56.230
 And he didn't say anything about running out of water.

00:47:56.230 --> 00:47:59.520
 And then I heard about it from some mutual friends.

00:47:59.520 --> 00:48:07.510
 And then when I saw him a couple of months later on, he, I said, Bob, why didn't you tell me that you ran out of water?

00:48:07.510 --> 00:48:11.510
 He said, well, I didn't want to introduce any negativity in our conversation.

00:48:11.510 --> 00:48:14.510
 So I guess drought is negativity.

00:48:14.510 --> 00:48:16.510
 It's kind of like minus.

00:48:16.510 --> 00:48:23.570
 It's the sort of absence of water or there's less water available than we'd like to have or that we need.

00:48:23.570 --> 00:48:31.140
 When I first moved here, I started hearing rumors that, you know, so and so, they're trucking in water now and that kind of thing.

00:48:31.140 --> 00:48:35.200
 But no one would talk about it on their own farm or on their own vineyard.

00:48:35.200 --> 00:48:42.190
 And it's almost like if, if not talking about it in public would make it so you didn't have to do anything about it.

00:48:42.190 --> 00:48:44.190
 I think maybe that's changing now, though.

00:48:44.190 --> 00:48:52.890
 I did write something fairly recently for the Point Reyes Light about drought and water situation there.

00:48:52.890 --> 00:49:00.890
 And there are people in places in West Marin who do have water trucked in like there's a lot of people in Marshall.

00:49:00.890 --> 00:49:08.370
 But they're not, they're not really advertising the fact that they're trucking in water.

00:49:08.370 --> 00:49:23.200
 I did have to do a fair amount of digging to, you know, get people to say, well, how much are you paying and where is the water coming from and how often does this happen?

00:49:23.200 --> 00:49:31.200
 So, and I, it's, yeah, I haven't, there is not a lot that's written about people trucking in water either.

00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:32.170
 Right.

00:49:32.170 --> 00:49:36.160
 There's a lot of different like pockets of the story that really don't get told.

00:49:36.160 --> 00:49:38.080
 Yeah.

00:49:38.080 --> 00:49:43.110
 It seems like a lot of, well, people are dealing with the drought now, but also a lot of reporting on the drought.

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:50.920
 It seems like it's, it's almost like it's reporting on the future and it's reporting on what people are fear or what they hope for.

00:49:50.920 --> 00:49:57.950
 Like what are, what are people, yeah, where are people's fears and hopes for, for what will happen with water here?

00:49:57.950 --> 00:50:11.490
 Well, the fears I believe are that water will run out, that water where they live, water will run out or that'll get to be really expensive.

00:50:11.490 --> 00:50:15.490
 Or, but then I think that they also have fear of flood.

00:50:15.490 --> 00:50:22.490
 I mean, we live in a place where there's been like severe flooding and we go back and forth from drought to flood.

00:50:22.490 --> 00:50:28.490
 So extremes of weather, I think are, are what's in store for us.

00:50:28.490 --> 00:50:48.640
 And, and I think one of the keys is to be resilient to whatever comes along, that weather's going to be increasingly unpredictable, that we have to be able to survive and cope and, and store water and have more groundwater and conserve more and save rainwater.

00:50:48.640 --> 00:51:12.250
 Uh, learning how to do with less, um, um, and the hope, I, I think the hope is that some people, some people with imagination will be able to come up with some new ideas about how to, how to live, uh, uh, with a very scarce and finite resource.

00:51:12.250 --> 00:51:15.000
 Great, Jenna.

00:51:15.000 --> 00:51:16.180
 Well, thanks so much.

00:51:16.180 --> 00:51:23.180
 I just wanted to, um, if people want to read some of what you've written, where would you recommend that they start?

00:51:23.180 --> 00:51:25.180
 Where would you recommend that they go?

00:51:25.180 --> 00:51:27.470
 Oh, I guess I would say field days.

00:51:27.470 --> 00:51:29.470
 I think that's a good place to begin, really.

00:51:29.470 --> 00:51:36.470
 It's a good introduction to farming and agriculture and food and people who, the people who live here.

00:51:36.470 --> 00:51:47.890
 And I think everybody I wrote about is, is still involved with growing food or making food and, and cooking and, and, and trying new things and different things.

00:51:47.890 --> 00:51:50.890
 And, and, and they're keeping on going.

00:51:50.890 --> 00:51:52.330
 Yeah.

00:51:52.330 --> 00:51:53.520
 Awesome.

00:51:53.520 --> 00:51:54.520
 Thanks so much.

00:51:54.520 --> 00:51:55.520
 I really appreciate it.

00:51:55.520 --> 00:51:56.520
 Thank you very much.

00:51:56.520 --> 00:51:57.680
 Thanks.

